468138230965e-06,-0.09846394509077072,0.0,-0.0006003961316309869,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,-3.576278118089249e-07,0.0,0.0,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,0.0,-1.311301275563892e-06,-3.3378546504536644e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,46254,198,59,26056,1242,15159,16,1124,273,502,366,600,92,1124,37018,90,16,15170,17,47822,72,488,502,38154,59,60,2870,279,2629,374,4429,916,678,6785,25780,400,72,3,323,400,73,3,1741,429,400,16,1124,273,502,366,600,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nCompute\n\\[\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}},\\]where the sum is taken over all positive integers $i$ and $j$ such that $1 \\le j < i.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"a1e480bf649c245cf1ae35df0f7604cca1f570f5a9bfd8d8f151b6c15b122cfe"},"seconds":49.93596005439758,"seed":1128429996439439045,"stop":"eos","text":"To compute the sum \\(\\sum_{1 \\le j < i} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}}\\), we need to sum over all pairs \\((i, j)\\) where \\(i\\) and \\(j\\) are positive integers with \\(1 \\le j < i\\).\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the sum in a more manageable form by fixing \\(i\\) and summing over \\(j\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^{i + j}}\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out the term \\(\\frac{1}{2^i}\\) from the inner sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} \\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to evaluate the inner sum \\(\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j}\\). This is a geometric series with the first term \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) and common ratio \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{i-1} \\frac{1}{2^j} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} (1 - (\\frac{1}{2})^{i-1})}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\n\\]\n\nSo, the sum becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{2^{i-1}}\\right) = \\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} - \\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}}\n\\]\n\nWe can separate the sums:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} = \\sum_{i=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{i+2}} = \\frac{1}{4} \\sum_{i=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^i} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot 2 = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nFor the second sum:\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{2^{2i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{i=2}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{4^{i-1}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{4^k} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1/4}{3/4} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the original sum is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{3}{6} - \\frac{1}{6} = \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
